"""
# One value, one store: the concurrent-backend degradation class

**Category:** Interpreter concurrency bridge (similar-problem detection)
**Bugs:**
- doc/08_tracking/bug/mutex_rwlock_text_value_nulled_by_pure_std_backend_2026-07-28.md
- doc/08_tracking/bug/native_concurrent_backend_spawn_not_backend_aware_join_is_2026-08-04.md

## Overview

Those two bugs were filed nine days apart against different files and read as
unrelated. They are the same fault: **a concurrent operation whose halves
disagree about where the value lives, degrading silently instead of failing.**

| filed as | the disagreement |
|---|---|
| Mutex/RwLock null `text` | `value_to_runtime` handled Int/Float/Bool and mapped everything else to `RuntimeValue::NIL` — the value went into the runtime object and could not come back out |
| native `spawn`/`join` mismatch | `spawn` had no backend branch and wrote `THREAD_RESULTS`; `join` had one and read the native provider — write store and read store were different objects |

Neither raised an error. The first returned `nil`, the second returned `nil`,
and the pure_std join miss even defaulted to `Value::Int(0)` — three different
silent substitutes for "I lost your value".

The single-shape reproducing specs
(`lock_value_fidelity_pure_std_spec.spl`, `spawn_join_backend_store_spec.spl`)
pin the two observed cases. This spec sweeps the class, so a fix that repairs
only the observed shapes cannot pass:

| axis | members |
|---|---|
| value type | `text`, `i64`, `bool`, `f64`, array — the reported bug only nulled non-primitives, so primitives are the controls |
| primitive | Mutex, RwLock, thread spawn/join |
| backend | `pure_std` (the default) and `native` — the two bugs each broke exactly one, in opposite directions |

## Why a subprocess

`rt_set_concurrent_backend` mutates a **process-global** registry. Flipping it
inside the shared spec-runner process would change Map/Channel/Thread behaviour
for every other spec in the same run — the reason the 2026-07-28 report
explicitly declined to apply the `native` backend as a workaround. The sweep
therefore runs in a child process, and the fixture is driven through the
tree-walk interpreter (`SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpreter`), which is the only
engine that reaches `interpreter_extern/`; `run` alone is the Cranelift JIT and
would make this spec vacuous.

## Oracle

Every expected value is a distinct literal, and none is `nil` or `0` — the two
values the defects substituted. Assertions compare against those literals, never
against a second value that travelled the same path, because under the defects
both sides were `nil` and would have compared equal.
"""

use std.spec.*
use std.io_runtime.{file_write, process_run, env_get}

# NOTE: no `{...}` interpolation in the embedded source — a `{name}` inside this
# spec's own string literal is resolved by the SPEC's lexer and kills the file
# with `zero-examples` before any example runs.
val SWEEP_SOURCE: text = ("extern fn rt_mutex_new(initial: Any) -> Any\n" +
    "extern fn rt_mutex_lock(m: Any) -> Any\n" +
    "extern fn rt_rwlock_new(initial: Any) -> Any\n" +
    "extern fn rt_rwlock_read(l: Any) -> Any\n" +
    "extern fn rt_thread_spawn_isolated(closure: Any) -> i64\n" +
    "extern fn rt_thread_join(handle: i64) -> Any\n" +
    "extern fn rt_set_concurrent_backend(name: text)\n" +
    "extern fn rt_get_concurrent_backend() -> text\n" +
    "\n" +
    "fn sweep(tag: text):\n" +
    "    print(tag)\n" +
    "    print(rt_get_concurrent_backend())\n" +
    "    print(rt_mutex_lock(rt_mutex_new(\"mtext\")))\n" +
    "    print(rt_mutex_lock(rt_mutex_new(41)))\n" +
    "    print(rt_mutex_lock(rt_mutex_new(true)))\n" +
    "    print(rt_mutex_lock(rt_mutex_new(2.5)))\n" +
    "    print(rt_rwlock_read(rt_rwlock_new(\"rtext\")))\n" +
    "    print(rt_rwlock_read(rt_rwlock_new(43)))\n" +
    "    print(rt_thread_join(rt_thread_spawn_isolated(\\: 47)))\n" +
    "    print(rt_thread_join(rt_thread_spawn_isolated(\\: \"stext\")))\n" +
    "\n" +
    "fn main():\n" +
    "    rt_set_concurrent_backend(\"pure_std\")\n" +
    "    sweep(\"SWEEP-PURESTD\")\n" +
    "    rt_set_concurrent_backend(\"native\")\n" +
    "    sweep(\"SWEEP-NATIVE\")\n" +
    "    rt_set_concurrent_backend(\"pure_std\")\n" +
    "    print(\"END\")\n")

# Each backend's block, in the fixed print order of `sweep`. A wrong value in
# any slot breaks the contiguous match, so one assertion covers the whole row.
val EXPECTED_ROW: text = ("mtext\n41\ntrue\n2.5\nrtext\n43\n47\nstext")

fn run_sweep() -> text:
    val dir = "build/test-artifacts/concurrent_backend_store_parity"
    val src = dir + "/sweep_fixture.spl"
    var bin = env_get("SIMPLE_BIN")
    if bin == "":
        bin = "bin/simple"
    process_run("sh", ["-c", "mkdir -p " + dir])
    file_write(src, SWEEP_SOURCE)
    val cmd = ("SIMPLE_BOOTSTRAP=1 SIMPLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=0 " +
        "SIMPLE_EXECUTION_MODE=interpreter nice -n 19 " + bin +
        " run " + src + " 2>/dev/null || echo SWEEP-FAILED")
    val out = process_run("sh", ["-c", cmd])
    out.0

describe "every value type survives every lock and every backend":

    it "returns the stored value for each type, primitive and backend":
        # ONE example on purpose: the sweep costs a full compiler start-up and
        # spec module globals do not persist between examples, so splitting it
        # would mean one subprocess per row.
        step("Run the two-backend sweep in a child process, interpreted")
        val out = run_sweep()

        step("Refuse to pass on a run that produced nothing")
        # Without this a SWEEP-FAILED string satisfies every `not contains`
        # assertion below and the spec is vacuously green.
        expect(out.contains("SWEEP-FAILED")).to_equal(false)
        expect(out).to_contain("END")

        step("The fixture really switched backends — otherwise both rows are pure_std")
        # This is the anti-tautology control for the backend axis: without it a
        # no-op rt_set_concurrent_backend would let one working backend satisfy
        # both rows.
        expect(out).to_contain("SWEEP-PURESTD\npure_std")
        expect(out).to_contain("SWEEP-NATIVE\nnative")

        step("pure_std: text, int, bool, float, rwlock and spawn/join all intact")
        expect(out).to_contain("SWEEP-PURESTD\npure_std\n" + EXPECTED_ROW)

        step("native: the same row, from the same operations")
        expect(out).to_contain("SWEEP-NATIVE\nnative\n" + EXPECTED_ROW)

        step("Neither silent substitute — nil or 0 — appears anywhere in the sweep")
        # `nil` was the Mutex/RwLock and native-join substitute; `0` was the
        # pure_std join-miss substitute. Both are absent from every expected
        # value above, so any occurrence is a degradation.
        expect(out.contains("\nnil")).to_equal(false)
        expect(out.contains("\n0\n")).to_equal(false)
